WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.266 --> 00:00:06.433
<b>I'm Martin. We'll now look at the PrevCheck together from the pharmacy's perspective.</b>

2
00:00:06.433 --> 00:00:13.500
<b>The customer is standing in front of us with the booking code, having filled out the questionnaire. We now go to</b>

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.733
<b>prevcheck.ch, into the pharmacy portal, and look up the PrevCheck under 'Measurements'.</b>

4
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:22.766
<b>There you can enter or select the booking code.</b>

5
00:00:22.766 --> 00:00:27.533
<b>First, there's a safety question: does the customer feel healthy enough to take the test?</b>

6
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:36.400
<b>Or do they have acute symptoms? You can also see whether the PrevCheck has already been paid for online in advance.</b>

7
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:43.266
<b>Here we see the full process. We start with the blood analysis. Depending on the device, this takes 6 to 8 minutes.</b>

8
00:00:44.299 --> 00:00:48.866
<b>You measure the lipid panel for cholesterol and triglycerides in millimoles per litre,</b>

9
00:00:48.866 --> 00:00:53.400
<b>the long-term blood sugar HbA1c in percent, the weight, and the blood pressure.</b>

10
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.766
<b>Blood pressure can of course also be measured several times.</b>

11
00:00:57.766 --> 00:01:04.366
<b>We recommend that the customer generally be fasting. If the triglyceride levels are elevated and the customer is not fasting,</b>

12
00:01:04.366 --> 00:01:08.166
<b>a follow-up measurement may be useful. We'll come back to that later.</b>

13
00:01:09.966 --> 00:01:13.166
<b>If you only have one device, you need to follow the manufacturer's instructions</b>

14
00:01:13.166 --> 00:01:15.566
<b>as to whether you're allowed to fill the cassettes at the same time.</b>

15
00:01:15.566 --> 00:01:21.066
<b>Normally, you should wait until the device is free again before filling the second cassette.</b>

16
00:01:21.066 --> 00:01:24.066
<b>The filled cassettes shouldn't sit around for long.</b>

17
00:01:24.066 --> 00:01:28.133
<b>If the readings are outside the measuring range, or if 'n/a' is displayed,</b>

18
00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:32.566
<b>you can enter that in the form the same way. There's also a dropdown on the left-hand side for this.</b>

19
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.699
<b>For example, if the triglyceride levels are above 4.52 mmol/l,</b>

20
00:01:37.699 --> 00:01:40.766
<b>the device can no longer calculate the LDL values.</b>

21
00:01:41.266 --> 00:01:46.833
<b>If you enter it this way, an additional field for total cholesterol appears.</b>

22
00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:49.666
<b>You can fill that in, and the rest happens automatically.</b>

23
00:01:49.666 --> 00:01:53.766
<b>Once everything is entered, it's best to review everything once more and confirm it.</b>

24
00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:59.633
<b>The report is available immediately and is also sent by e-mail at that moment.</b>

25
00:01:59.633 --> 00:02:03.433
<b>The report is what the customer ultimately receives,</b>

26
00:02:03.433 --> 00:02:10.266
<b>either by e-mail or printed out. Let's now look at the report together, since it forms the basis for the consultation.</b>

27
00:02:10.266 --> 00:02:17.166
<b>This is a report. They're all structured the same way, but the content is tailored to the customer. The report has five parts.</b>

28
00:02:17.166 --> 00:02:25.100
<b>In the first part, you see an overview of diet, exercise/sport, family history, and substance use.</b>

29
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:32.033
<b>The assessment is based on the answers from the questionnaire. This helps you quickly get an overview of the starting situation.</b>

30
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:37.100
<b>The second part covers the lab results – what you measured.</b>

31
00:02:38.633 --> 00:02:42.566
<b>BMI, long-term blood sugar (HbA1c), cholesterol, triglycerides, and blood pressure.</b>

32
00:02:42.566 --> 00:02:46.500
<b>Each value is rated with a colour, and there's a short explanation for each.</b>

33
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.600
<b>We recommend first briefly describing the overview and the measurements,</b>

34
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.799
<b>and then going into detail using the risk score or individual values.</b>

35
00:03:00.066 --> 00:03:28.466
<b>Now it's about the risk assessment. We have two scores. The first score is for cardiovascular risk, based on SCORE2 – the current risk score for Europe. Here you see the 10-year risk of a cardiovascular event such as a stroke, a target value, and a heart-age estimate. We also show the age-related baseline risk. A 75-year-old who runs marathons and does everything right can still fall into the orange or red zone.</b>

36
00:03:28.466 --> 00:03:30.866
<b>Age is simply a major risk factor.</b>

37
00:03:30.866 --> 00:03:38.000
<b>The second score is a diabetes score based on FINDRISC. It consists of risk points and a target value.</b>

38
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.100
<b>The more points, the worse it is. The target value is also tailored to the individual.</b>

39
00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:49.100
<b>On the right-hand side, you also see the health goals and what the person can do to improve their situation.</b>

40
00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:56.266
<b>If the HbA1c value is already elevated, i.e. above 5.7%, the score is not displayed,</b>

41
00:03:56.266 --> 00:04:01.733
<b>because the risk has already materialised. The person is then in the diabetes or prediabetes range.</b>

42
00:04:01.733 --> 00:04:05.033
<b>In that case, we recommend that the person sees a doctor.</b>

43
00:04:07.166 --> 00:04:15.433
<b>Both risk assessments can also trigger warnings. For example, if a person indicates they've already had a heart attack,</b>

44
00:04:15.433 --> 00:04:21.566
<b>the report will note that SCORE2 is not clinically validated for such high-risk patients.</b>

45
00:04:21.566 --> 00:04:26.233
<b>The fourth part covers lifestyle measures. We've deliberately kept this compact.</b>

46
00:04:26.233 --> 00:04:37.066
<b>It covers diet, sun exposure, smoking, exercise, and alcohol. This is of course tailored to the customer. Anyone looking for more information will find further details in the Info Centre.</b>

47
00:04:37.066 --> 00:04:41.266
<b>For example, how to adjust your diet if you have elevated cholesterol levels.</b>

48
00:04:41.266 --> 00:04:52.666
<b>The last part covers recommended preventive measures. This is based on eviPrev, an association of physicians who examined which preventive measures make sense for the Swiss population.</b>

49
00:04:52.666 --> 00:05:02.100
<b>Depending on the risk profile, this can be a longer or shorter list. If someone has smoked, is overweight, and has a family history, the list can be correspondingly long.</b>

50
00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:07.733
<b>At the end of the report, there's also general information, the pharmacy's logo, and address.</b>

51
00:05:07.733 --> 00:05:12.666
<b>If you print it out, you can also sign it, so it's clear who carried out the measurements.</b>

52
00:05:12.666 --> 00:05:16.400
<b>In the case of the InStore PrevCheck, we don't have the customer's e-mail address.</b>

53
00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:24.633
<b>That's why, on the left-hand side, there's a QR code the customer can scan to retrieve the report, or on the right-hand side, the option to enter their e-mail address</b>

54
00:05:24.633 --> 00:05:30.900
<b>and have the report sent to them. You can also set a reminder for a possible follow-up measurement.</b>

55
00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:39.966
<b>Briefly on follow-up measurements: if the triglyceride levels are elevated, i.e. above 4.52 mmol per litre, a warning appears in the report.</b>

56
00:05:39.966 --> 00:05:46.500
<b>If the customer wasn't fasting and the levels are elevated, a follow-up measurement may be worthwhile.</b>

57
00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:54.933
<b>A follow-up measurement works like this: the customer comes to the pharmacy a second time – this time fasting – and the measurement is repeated. The customer pays for this at the pharmacy as usual.</b>

58
00:05:54.933 --> 00:05:59.466
<b>With the new values, the report can be reissued at the follow-up measurement, replacing the old report.</b>

59
00:05:59.466 --> 00:06:10.666
<b>All values are recalculated, and the customer receives a clean, updated risk assessment.</b>

60
00:06:10.666 --> 00:06:17.466
<b>That's how you carry out the PrevCheck from the pharmacy's perspective. Now Christian will take over again and show you more about our platform.</b>
